TRACE WORKFLOW FOR MCP AGENTS

Use trace for evidence about syscalls, process switches, timer and
kernel events. It has overhead and finite buffering.

SHORT, LOW-NOISE INVESTIGATION
  ps
  trace on -s
  hello
  trace show
  trace on
Default trace is enabled in recent builds; 'trace show' stops it.
Always restore the requested trace mode after collecting output.

EVENT LEVELS
  trace on       default/standard event selection
  trace on -s    syscall-only capture
  trace on -a    all supported events
  trace off      stop capture
  trace show [PID]    bounded snapshot of current buffer
  trace run -s hello  trace one command, may exceed MCP limits
  trace run -a hello  adds non-syscall contexts; higher volume
  trace follow [PID] continuous view (NEVER via MCP)

OUTPUT MANAGEMENT
  trace show PID       filter output to a known PID
  trace on -s          less noisy capture than -a
  trace show           finite, but may still exceed 4096 output
Use terminal directly if the trace result is too large.
Do not assume host HTTP failure indicates the traced command failed.

INTERPRETATION
  ENTER and EXIT identify call boundaries.
  Ret identifies returned number, often negative on error.
  SWITCH relates scheduler transitions, not user-level function call.
  TIMER events may dominate -a captures.
  Symbol names in /proc/sym/kernel can help map kernel addresses.
Trace alters timing. Intermittent bugs can change under tracing.

DEBUGGING LOOP
  1) reproduce without trace;
  2) take a syscall-only trace;
  3) identify unexpected return or missing expected syscall;
  4) correlate with lsof, ps, bounded dumps;
  5) restore trace mode; report observed facts vs hypotheses.
